. ** UPDATE: Hypothesis test using male partner's share in various domestic wor
> k during WEEKDAYS as the only dependent variable. We only employ fixed effect
> s model instead of employing both fixed and random effects models **  
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * 
.         
.         /* We consider the male partner's share in domestic work during weekd
> ays as a good measure for gender equality in the household. We use three vari
> ables denoting hours spent during weekdays in childcare, housework, and repai
> r work, respectively (pli0044_h, pli0043_h, pli0049_h). We add the hours spen
> t in the three domains together and calculate for each household the male par
> tner's share as our dependent variable, ranging from 0 to 100: A higher share
>  indicates more gender equality in HH.
>         
>         We run fixed effects linear regresion analysis with domstcwd_rt as de
> pendent variable, applying robust standard errors. Our independent variable k
> idsex_all has three categories denoting whether having biological daughters i
> n the survey year: `No daughter', `Only daughter(s), and `Mixed'. 
>         
>         We also include the following control variables: age group, education
> , employment status, net household income, marital status, number of biologic
> al children, whether youngest biological child <= 3 years, region (West or Ea
> st Germany). Gender and migraiton background as time-invariant variables are 
> not included.
>         */
.         
.         * Run analysis at household level
.         //Only keep male partner in HH to account for his socio-demographics
.         keep if sex == 1
(8,128 observations deleted)

.         
.         xtset pid syear

